# Surfaces

This is a multi-surface Ruby on Rails application with three user-facing surfaces:

- `app` — end-user application; Palm is its browser relying party
- `org` — staff / organization surface; Tech is its global operator relying party
- `com` — public / corporate surface

Tech and Palm remain independent relying-party boundaries within their respective surfaces. Tech
uses operator identity and requires its own `tech.access` capability. Palm uses client identity.
Neither surface inherits another surface's credentials, permissions, or sessions.

Each surface is an independent boundary.

## Do not mix surfaces

Do not share controllers, routes, views, policies, sessions, or state across surfaces unless the
existing code has an explicit, reviewed shared abstraction. One surface must not access another
surface directly.

## Per-surface host constraints

- Each surface must enforce its own host constraints.
- Never assume a default host.
- Cross-surface routing is forbidden.

## Boundary discipline

- Respect the boundary in every layer: request handling, authorization, and persistence.
- Do not store request data so that it can leak across surfaces (see `absolute-rules` on shared
  state).
